1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,996
At the college canteen, a cup of tea
costs fifteen rupees and a samosa

2
00:00:04,996 --> 00:00:06,029
costs twenty.

3
00:00:06,429 --> 00:00:09,285
For x friends, the tea costs fifteen x.

4
00:00:09,285 --> 00:00:11,186
The samosas cost twenty x.

5
00:00:11,636 --> 00:00:14,578
Diya does not add the
bill friend by friend.

6
00:00:14,578 --> 00:00:16,250
She adds the two rules.

7
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:19,498
Fifteen x plus twenty x is thirty five x.

8
00:00:19,498 --> 00:00:22,387
One new rule, for any number of friends.

9
00:00:22,887 --> 00:00:27,321
Adding two rules to make a new
rule: the algebra of real functions.

10
00:00:27,971 --> 00:00:31,631
Take two real functions, f
and g, on the same domain.

11
00:00:31,981 --> 00:00:37,764
Their sum, f plus g, adds the two
outputs at each x: f of x plus g of x.

12
00:00:38,164 --> 00:00:39,329
Watch it live.

13
00:00:39,329 --> 00:00:40,989
f of x is x squared.

14
00:00:40,989 --> 00:00:43,031
g of x is two x plus one.

15
00:00:43,431 --> 00:00:46,757
At every x, stack the
height of g on top of f.

16
00:00:46,757 --> 00:00:48,948
The new height traces f plus g.

17
00:00:49,348 --> 00:00:54,238
The result, x squared plus two x
plus one, is x plus one, all squared:

18
00:00:54,238 --> 00:00:56,728
the same U, shifted one step left.

19
00:00:57,378 --> 00:01:00,794
NCERT defines five ways
to combine f and g.

20
00:01:01,144 --> 00:01:02,044
One.

21
00:01:01,736 --> 00:01:02,636
The sum.

22
00:01:02,630 --> 00:01:06,263
f plus g, at x, is f of x plus g of x.

23
00:01:07,013 --> 00:01:07,913
Two.

24
00:01:07,605 --> 00:01:08,904
The difference.

25
00:01:08,904 --> 00:01:12,651
f minus g, at x, is f of x minus g of x.

26
00:01:13,401 --> 00:01:14,301
Three.

27
00:01:14,074 --> 00:01:15,543
A scalar multiple.

28
00:01:15,543 --> 00:01:20,226
Alpha f, at x, is alpha times
f of x, for any real alpha.

29
00:01:20,976 --> 00:01:21,876
Four.

30
00:01:21,613 --> 00:01:22,718
The product.

31
00:01:22,718 --> 00:01:25,985
f g, at x, is f of x times g of x.

32
00:01:26,735 --> 00:01:27,635
Five.

33
00:01:27,393 --> 00:01:28,592
The quotient.

34
00:01:28,592 --> 00:01:35,080
f over g, at x, is f of x over g of
x, but only where g of x is not zero.

35
00:01:35,830 --> 00:01:37,019
Side by side.

36
00:01:37,019 --> 00:01:41,358
The product f g works at every
x where both f and g work.

37
00:01:41,758 --> 00:01:44,220
The quotient needs one more check.

38
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:47,588
At x equals minus one
half, g of x is zero.

39
00:01:47,588 --> 00:01:49,570
So f over g breaks there.

40
00:01:50,170 --> 00:01:51,124
Your turn.

41
00:01:51,124 --> 00:01:54,834
f of x is x squared, g
of x is x minus three.

42
00:01:54,834 --> 00:01:57,160
Is f over g defined at three?

43
00:01:57,160 --> 00:01:58,565
Pause and decide.

44
00:02:01,765 --> 00:02:02,665
No!

45
00:02:02,274 --> 00:02:06,340
g of three is zero, and we
can never divide by zero.

46
00:02:06,990 --> 00:02:08,020
Remember this.

47
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:10,050
Combine the outputs at each x.

48
00:02:10,050 --> 00:02:13,918
For a quotient, throw out every
x where the bottom is zero.

49
00:02:14,618 --> 00:02:16,391
Now, comparing functions.

50
00:02:16,391 --> 00:02:19,261
This goes beyond NCERT, but JEE needs it.

51
00:02:19,611 --> 00:02:24,183
Two functions are equal only when they
have the same domain, and the same

52
00:02:24,183 --> 00:02:25,553
output at every x.

53
00:02:25,953 --> 00:02:28,295
Compare x, with x squared over x.

54
00:02:28,295 --> 00:02:30,889
Cancel the x, and they look the same.

55
00:02:31,289 --> 00:02:34,364
But x squared over x
is not defined at zero.

56
00:02:34,364 --> 00:02:36,865
Its graph has a hole at the origin.

57
00:02:36,865 --> 00:02:38,529
So they are not equal.

58
00:02:39,129 --> 00:02:40,029
Quick one.

59
00:02:39,957 --> 00:02:43,971
Are mod x, and the square root
of x squared, equal functions?

60
00:02:43,971 --> 00:02:45,140
Pause and think.

61
00:02:48,340 --> 00:02:49,240
Yes!

62
00:02:48,885 --> 00:02:53,345
Both have domain R, and both
give the same output at every x.

63
00:02:53,995 --> 00:02:55,730
Now, one worked example.

64
00:02:56,080 --> 00:03:01,204
Let f of x be root x, and g
of x be x, for x zero or more.

65
00:03:01,204 --> 00:03:04,327
Find f plus g, f g, and f over g.

66
00:03:04,827 --> 00:03:05,738
Step one.

67
00:03:05,738 --> 00:03:08,739
f plus g, at x, is root x plus x.

68
00:03:09,489 --> 00:03:10,389
Step two.

69
00:03:10,309 --> 00:03:12,747
f g, at x, is root x times x.

70
00:03:12,747 --> 00:03:15,435
That is x to the power three over two.

71
00:03:16,185 --> 00:03:17,172
Step three.

72
00:03:17,172 --> 00:03:20,069
f over g, at x, is root x over x.

73
00:03:20,069 --> 00:03:22,012
That is one over root x.

74
00:03:22,762 --> 00:03:23,715
Step four.

75
00:03:23,715 --> 00:03:28,766
The bottom must not be zero, so f
over g lives only where x is greater

76
00:03:28,766 --> 00:03:29,719
than zero.

77
00:03:30,369 --> 00:03:31,835
Now, the exam trap.

78
00:03:32,185 --> 00:03:36,395
f of x is root x, and g of
x is root of one minus x.

79
00:03:36,395 --> 00:03:39,549
Many students say f
plus g works everywhere.

80
00:03:39,949 --> 00:03:42,946
But f plus g needs both at the same x.

81
00:03:42,946 --> 00:03:45,202
Root x needs x zero or more.

82
00:03:45,202 --> 00:03:48,309
Root of one minus x needs x one or less.

83
00:03:48,309 --> 00:03:50,619
So the domain is zero to one.

84
00:03:51,269 --> 00:03:52,623
Where is it asked?

85
00:03:52,623 --> 00:03:57,238
In JEE Main, as questions that combine
two functions and ask for the domain

86
00:03:57,238 --> 00:03:58,339
of the result.

87
00:03:58,939 --> 00:04:00,231
Exam level now.

88
00:04:00,231 --> 00:04:02,729
What is the domain of f over g?

89
00:04:02,729 --> 00:04:04,077
Pause and solve.

90
00:04:09,477 --> 00:04:10,743
The answer is C.

91
00:04:11,243 --> 00:04:14,698
f needs four minus x
squared to be zero or more.

92
00:04:14,698 --> 00:04:17,130
So x runs from minus two to two.

93
00:04:17,630 --> 00:04:21,194
g works for every x, so it
adds no limit of its own.

94
00:04:21,694 --> 00:04:25,521
But g of x is zero at x
equals one and minus one.

95
00:04:25,521 --> 00:04:27,049
Those two must go.

96
00:04:27,549 --> 00:04:31,800
So the domain is minus two to two,
including the ends, but without minus

97
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:32,734
one and one.

98
00:04:33,334 --> 00:04:34,793
Here is your recap.

99
00:04:34,793 --> 00:04:36,292
Screenshot this card.

100
00:04:39,492 --> 00:04:42,329
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

101
00:04:42,729 --> 00:04:43,629
One.

102
00:04:43,308 --> 00:04:48,875
If f of x is x plus two, and g of
x is three x, what is f plus g?

103
00:04:49,775 --> 00:04:50,675
Two.

104
00:04:50,377 --> 00:04:55,218
If g of x is x minus five,
where is f over g not defined?

105
00:04:56,118 --> 00:04:57,018
Three.

106
00:04:56,818 --> 00:05:00,538
Are x, and x squared
over x, equal functions?

107
00:05:00,538 --> 00:05:01,438
Why?

108
00:05:02,224 --> 00:05:05,164
Next time, we leave functions for angles.

109
00:05:05,164 --> 00:05:09,189
Why measure a bicycle wheel's
turn by the distance it rolls?

110
00:05:09,189 --> 00:05:11,108
Degree and radian measures.
